---
import Base from '../../components/Base.astro';
const guides=[
['why-cup-of-flour-weighs-different-amounts','Why a cup of flour weighs different amounts','Flour compacts when scooped directly from a bag, so the same cup can contain different amounts. Fluff flour first, spoon it into a dry cup, and level the top without tapping. Different milling styles and moisture levels also affect weight. A digital scale measures mass directly and is more repeatable for baking.'],
['how-to-measure-flour','How to measure flour: spoon and level vs scoop','Loosen flour with a spoon, gently spoon it into a dry measuring cup, then sweep a straight edge across the top. Avoid shaking, tapping, or pressing it down. Scooping with the cup often compacts flour and adds more than the recipe intended. When a recipe provides grams, weighing is the most consistent approach.'],
['grams-vs-cups','Grams vs cups: which is more accurate for baking','Grams measure weight while cups measure volume. Ingredients have different densities, so a cup of sugar, flour, and honey cannot share one conversion factor. Weight measurements are easier to reproduce across kitchens and useful for scaled recipes. Cups remain convenient, but measuring method matters.'],
['packed-vs-loose-brown-sugar','Packed vs loose brown sugar','Brown sugar is moist and clumps together, so many recipes call for it to be packed. Press it into the cup until it holds the cup shape when turned out. A loose measure contains more air and usually weighs less. Follow the recipe wording because moisture and brand affect the result.'],
['us-metric-uk-cups','US cup vs metric cup vs UK cup','A US customary cup is about 236.6 milliliters, while a metric cup is commonly 250 milliliters. Australian tablespoons are commonly 20 milliliters, compared with about 14.8 milliliters in the US system. Check the recipe origin and select the matching measuring system.'],
['tablespoons-in-a-cup','How many tablespoons are in a cup','In US customary measures, one cup contains 16 tablespoons and one tablespoon contains 3 teaspoons. Metric and Australian tablespoon sizes can differ. These are volume relationships; converting to grams still depends on the ingredient. Use ingredient-specific values rather than one generic grams-per-tablespoon number.'],
['butter-conversions','Butter conversions: sticks, cups, grams, tablespoons','One standard US stick of butter is commonly 8 tablespoons, one-half cup, or about 113 grams. A full cup is about 227 grams. Package sizes can differ by country. If a recipe calls for melted butter, check whether the amount refers to butter before or after melting.'],
['liquids-ml-grams','Converting liquids: why milliliters and grams differ','Milliliters measure volume and grams measure mass. One milliliter of water is close to one gram, but oil, milk, honey, and syrups have different densities. Use a liquid measuring jug for volume and a kitchen scale for mass. Do not assume every liquid converts one-to-one.'],
['sifted-vs-unsifted-flour','Sifted vs unsifted flour: when it matters','Sifting loosens flour, removes lumps, and changes how much fits in a cup. A recipe that says one cup sifted flour usually means sift first and then measure; one cup flour, sifted can mean measure first and then sift. Wording conventions vary, so read the recipe carefully.'],
['kitchen-scale-calibration','How to use and calibrate a kitchen scale','Place the scale on a firm, level surface and check that it reads zero before adding ingredients. Use tare to subtract the bowl weight. Follow the manufacturer instructions for calibration; not every household scale supports user calibration. Keep the platform clean and replace low batteries if readings drift.'],
['baking-powder-soda','Baking powder and baking soda measurements','Baking powder and baking soda are different ingredients and are not interchangeable in equal amounts. Baking soda needs an acidic ingredient to react as intended. Small errors can affect taste, browning, and rise, so use a level spoon or a scale that measures small quantities reliably.'],
['common-conversion-mistakes','Common conversion mistakes in recipes','Common mistakes include treating all ingredients as if they have the same density, using a US cup for a metric recipe, packing flour when the recipe expects it spooned and leveled, and confusing cooked and uncooked weights. Identify the ingredient, measuring system, and preparation method first.']
];
export function getStaticPaths(){return guides.map(g=>({params:{slug:g[0]},props:{guide:g}}));}const {guide}=Astro.props;
---
<Base title={`${guide[1]} | CupToGram`} description={guide[2].slice(0,150)} path={`/guides/${guide[0]}/`}><main class="wrap"><article class="content"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> / <a href="/guides/">Guides</a> / {guide[1]}</div><span class="eyebrow">Baking guide</span><h1>{guide[1]}</h1><p class="lede">{guide[2]}</p><p>{guide[2]}</p><h2>Practical takeaway</h2><p>Use the measuring method specified in your recipe, choose the correct regional unit system, and use a kitchen scale when precision matters. Conversion values are estimates and can vary with ingredient brand and preparation.</p><p class="small muted">Editorial note: this introductory guide should be reviewed and expanded with reliable sources before launch as a final reference article.</p><a href="/methodology/">Read our measurement methodology →</a></article></main></Base>